  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14170937/assets/b6d0bd3fbb089a811a638b1115a5b5becb6ab9673567c227920ac60b0ec5fbfb.png)\n\nThe equation of the line shown above is $y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that our line passes through the points $(2,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{2}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+b \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(2)+b \\\\ 1 & =3+b \\\\ 1-3 & =b \\\\ -2 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\,x-2.$",
